first equation:\n\n\\[2x + 3y = 12\\]\n\\[2(y + 1) + 3y = 12\\]\n\nStep 3: Simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[2y + 2 + 3y = 12\\]\n\\[5y + 2 = 12\\]\n\\[5y = 10\\]\n\\[y = 2\\]\n\nStep 4: Substitute the value of \\(y\\) back into the expression we found for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x = y + 1\\]\n\\[x = 2 + 1\\]\n\\[x = 3\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = 3\\) and \\(y = 2\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(2x + 3y = 12\\)\n\\[2(3) + 3(2) = 6 + 6 = 12\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x - y = 1\\)\n\\[3 - 2 = 1\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>The solution to the system of equations \\(2x + 3y = 12\\) and \\(x - y = 1\\) using the substitution method is \\(x = 3\\) and \\(y = 2\\). <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's correct. Can you provide an example with more complex equations?\n<think>\nCertainly! Let's consider a more complex system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(3x - 2y = 7\\)\n2. \\(x + 4y = 10\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the second equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x + 4y = 10\\]\n\\[x = 10 - 4y\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(x\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[3x - 2y = 7\\]\n\\[3(10 - 4y) - 2y = 7\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[30 - 12y - 2y = 7\\]\n\\[30 - 14y = 7\\]\n\\[-14y = 7 - 30\\]\n\\[-14y = -23\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{23}{14}\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = \\frac{23}{14}\\) back into the expression we found for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x = 10 - 4y\\]\n\\[x = 10 - 4\\left(\\frac{23}{14}\\right)\\]\n\\[x = 10 - \\frac{92}{14}\\]\n\\[x = 10 - \\frac{46}{7}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{70}{7} - \\frac{46}{7}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{24}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{24}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{23}{14}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(3x - 2y = 7\\)\n\\[3\\left(\\frac{24}{7}\\right) - 2\\left(\\frac{23}{14}\\right) = \\frac{72}{7} - \\frac{46}{14} = \\frac{144}{14} - \\frac{46}{14} = \\frac{98}{14} = 7\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x + 4y = 10\\)\n\\[\\frac{24}{7} + 4\\left(\\frac{23}{14}\\right) = \\frac{24}{7} + \\frac{92}{14} = \\frac{48}{14} + \\frac{92}{14} = \\frac{140}{14} = 10\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(3x - 2y = 7\\) and \\(x + 4y = 10\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the second equation, solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[x + 4y = 10\\]\n\\[x = 10 - 4y\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = 10 - 4y\\) into the first equation:\n\\[3x - 2y = 7\\]\n\\[3(10 - 4y) - 2y = 7\\]\n\\[30 - 12y - 2y = 7\\]\n\\[30 - 14y = 7\\]\n\\[-14y = 7 - 30\\]\n\\[-14y = -23\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{23}{14}\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y = \\frac{23}{14}\\) back into \\(x = 10 - 4y\\):\n\\[x = 10 - 4\\left(\\frac{23}{14}\\right)\\]\n\\[x = 10 - \\frac{92}{14}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{140}{14} - \\frac{92}{14}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{48}{14}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{24}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{24}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{23}{14}\\).\n\nTo verify:\n1. \\(3x - 2y = 7\\)\n\\[3\\left(\\frac{24}{7}\\right) - 2\\left(\\frac{23}{14}\\right) = \\frac{72}{7} - \\frac{46}{14} = \\frac{144}{14} - \\frac{46}{14} = \\frac{98}{14} = 7\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x + 4y = 10\\)\n\\[\\frac{24}{7} + 4\\left(\\frac{23}{14}\\right) = \\frac{24}{7} + \\frac{92}{14} = \\frac{48}{14} + \\frac{92}{14} = \\frac{140}{14} = 10\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's very clear. Thank you for walking me through the process. Can you provide another example with a different system of equations?\n<think>\nCertainly! Let's consider a different system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(2x + y = 5\\)\n2. \\(x - 3y = -1\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the first equation for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[2x + y = 5\\]\n\\[y = 5 - 2x\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(y\\) into the 