 different system of equations to further practice the substitution method?\n<think>\nOf course! Let's consider another system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(4x - y = 5\\)\n2. \\(x + 3y = 11\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the second equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x + 3y = 11\\]\n\\[x = 11 - 3y\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(x\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[4x - y = 5\\]\n\\[4(11 - 3y) - y = 5\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[44 - 12y - y = 5\\]\n\\[44 - 13y = 5\\]\n\\[-13y = 5 - 44\\]\n\\[-13y = -39\\]\n\\[y = 3\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = 3\\) back into \\(x = 11 - 3y\\):\n\n\\[x = 11 - 3(3)\\]\n\\[x = 11 - 9\\]\n\\[x = 2\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = 2\\) and \\(y = 3\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(4x - y = 5\\)\n\\[4(2) - 3 = 8 - 3 = 5\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x + 3y = 11\\)\n\\[2 + 3(3) = 2 + 9 = 11\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(4x - y = 5\\) and \\(x + 3y = 11\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the second equation, solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[x + 3y = 11\\]\n\\[x = 11 - 3y\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = 11 - 3y\\) into the first equation:\n\\[4x - y = 5\\]\n\\[4(11 - 3y) - y = 5\\]\n\\[44 - 12y - y = 5\\]\n\\[44 - 13y = 5\\]\n\\[-13y = 5 - 44\\]\n\\[-13y = -39\\]\n\\[y = 3\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y = 3\\) back into \\(x = 11 - 3y\\):\n\\[x = 11 - 3(3)\\]\n\\[x = 11 - 9\\]\n\\[x = 2\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = 2\\) and \\(y = 3\\).\n\nTo verify:\n1. \\(4x - y = 5\\)\n\\[4(2) - 3 = 8 - 3 = 5\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x + 3y = 11\\)\n\\[2 + 3(3) = 2 + 9 = 11\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed examples. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to further practice the substitution method?\n<think>\nOf course! Let's consider another system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(x - 3y = -1\\)\n2. \\(2x + y = 8\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the first equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x - 3y = -1\\]\n\\[x = -1 + 3y\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(x\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[2x + y = 8\\]\n\\[2(-1 + 3y) + y = 8\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[2(-1) + 6y + y = 8\\]\n\\[-2 + 7y = 8\\]\n\\[7y = 8 + 2\\]\n\\[7y = 10\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{10}{7}\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = \\frac{10}{7}\\) back into \\(x = -1 + 3y\\):\n\n\\[x = -1 + 3\\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right)\\]\n\\[x = -1 + \\frac{30}{7}\\]\n\\[x = -\\frac{7}{7} + \\frac{30}{7}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{23}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{23}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{10}{7}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(x - 3y = -1\\)\n\\[\\frac{23}{7} - 3\\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) = \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{30}{7} = \\frac{-7}{7} = -1\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(2x + y = 8\\)\n\\[2\\left(\\frac{23}{7}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) = \\frac{46}{7} + \\frac{10}{7} = \\frac{56}{7} = 8\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(x - 3y = -1\\) and \\(2x + y = 8\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the first equation, solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[x - 3y = -1\\]\n\\[x = -1 + 3y\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = -1 + 3y\\) into the second equation:\n\\[2x + y = 8\\]\n\\[2(-1 + 3y) + y = 8\\]\n\\[2(-1) + 6y + y = 8\\]\n\\[-2 + 7y = 8\\]\n\\[7y = 8 + 2\\]\n\\[7y = 10\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{10}{7}\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y = \\frac{10}{7}\\) back into \\(x = -1 + 3y\\):\n\\[x = -1 + 3\\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right)\\]\n\\[x = -1 + \\frac{30}{7}\\]\n\\[x = -\\frac{7}{7} + \\frac{30}{7}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{23}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{23}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{10}{7}\\).\n\nTo verify:\n1. \\(x - 3y = -1\\)\n\\[\\frac{23}{7} - 3\\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) = \\frac{23}{7} - \\frac{30}{7} = \\frac{-7}{7} = -1\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(2x + y = 8\\)\n\\[2\\left(\\frac{23}{7}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) = \\frac{46}{7} + \\frac{10}{7} = \\frac{56}{7} = 8\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed examples. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to further practice the substitution method?\n<think>\nOf course! Let's consider another system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(x + y = 4\\)\n2. \\(2x - y = 2\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the first equation for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[x + y = 4\\]\n\\[y = 4 - x\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(y\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[2x - y = 2\\]\n\\[2x - (4 - x) = 2\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[2x - 4 + x = 2\\]\n\\[3x - 4 = 2\\]\n\\[3x = 2 + 4\\]\n\\[3x = 6\\]\n\\[x = 2\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(x = 2\\) back into \\(y = 4 - x\\):\n\n\\[y = 4 - 2\\]\n\\[y = 2\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = 2\\) and \\(y = 2\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(x + y = 4\\)\n\\[2 + 2 = 4\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(2x - y = 2\\)\n\\[2(2) - 2 = 4 - 2 = 2\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(x + y = 4\\) and \\(2x - y = 2\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the first equation, solve for \\(y\\):\n\\[x + y = 4\\]\n\\[y = 4 - x\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(y = 4 - x\\) into the second equation:\n\\[2x - y = 2\\]\n\\[2x - (4 - x) = 2\\]\n\\[2x - 4 + x = 2\\]\n\\[3x - 4 = 2\\]\n\\[3x = 2 + 4\\]\n\\[3x = 6\\]\n\\[x = 2\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(x = 2\\) back into \\(y = 4 - x\\):\n\\[y = 4 - 2\\]\n\\[y = 2\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = 2\\) and \\(y = 2\\).\n\nTo verify:\n1. \\(x + y = 4\\)\n\\[2 + 2 = 4\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(2x - y = 2\\)\n\\[2(2) - 2 = 4 - 2 = 2\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed examples. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to further practice the substitution method?\n<think>\nCertainly! Let's consider another system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(3x + 2y = 11\\)\n2. \\(x - y = 1\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the second equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x - y = 1\\]\n\\[x = 1 + y\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(x\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[3x + 2y = 11\\]\n\\[3(1 + y) + 2y = 11\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[3 + 3y + 2y = 11\\]\n\\[3 + 5y = 11\\]\n\\[5y = 11 - 3\\]\n\\[5y = 8\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{8}{5}\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = \\frac{8}{5}\\) back into \\(x = 1 + y\\):\n\n\\[x = 1 + \\frac{8}{5}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{5}{5} + \\frac{8}{5}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{13}{5}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{13}{5}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{8}{5}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(3x + 2y = 11\\)\n\\[3\\left(\\frac{13}{5}\\right) + 2\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right) = \\frac{39}{5} + \\frac{16}{5} = \\frac{55}{5} = 11\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x - y = 1\\)\n\\[\\frac{13}{5} - \\frac{8}{5} = \\frac{5}{5} = 1\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(3x + 2y = 11\\) and \\(x - y = 1\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the second equation, solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[x - y = 1\\]\n\\[x = 1 + y\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = 1 + y\\) into the first equation:\n\\[3x + 2y = 11\\]\n\\[3(1 + y) + 2y = 11\\]\n\\[3 + 3y + 2y = 11\\]\n\\[3 + 5y = 11\\]\n\\[5y = 11 - 3\\]\n\\[5y = 8\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{8}{5}\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y = \\frac{8}{5}\\) back into \\(x = 1 + y\\):\n\\[x = 1 + \\frac{8}{5}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{5}{5} + \\frac{8}{5}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{13}{5}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{13}{5}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{8}{5}\\).\n\nTo verify:\n1. \\(3x + 2y = 11\\)\n\\[3\\left(\\frac{13}{5}\\right) + 2\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right) = \\frac{39}{5} + \\frac{16}{5} = \\frac{55}{5} = 11\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x - y = 1\\)\n\\[\\frac{13}{5} - \\frac{8}{5} = \\frac{5}{5} = 1\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed examples. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to further practice the substitution method?\n<think>\nOf course! Let's consider another system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(x + 3y = 7\\)\n2. \\(2x - y = 4\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the second equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[2x - y = 4\\]\n\\[2x = 4 + y\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{4 + y}{2}\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(x\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[x + 3y = 7\\]\n\\[\\frac{4 + y}{2} + 3y = 7\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\\frac{4 + y}{2} + 3y = 7\\]\n\\[\\frac{4 + y + 6y}{2} = 7\\]\n\\[\\frac{4 + 7y}{2} = 7\\]\n\\[4 + 7y = 14\\]\n\\[7y = 14 - 4\\]\n\\[7y = 10\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{10}{7}\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = \\frac{10}{7}\\) back into \\(x = \\frac{4 + y}{2}\\):\n\n\\[x = \\frac{4 + \\frac{10}{7}}{2}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{\\frac{28}{7} + \\frac{10}{7}}{2}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{\\frac{38}{7}}{2}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{38}{14}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{19}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{19}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{10}{7}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(x + 3y = 7\\)\n\\[\\frac{19}{7} + 3\\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) = \\frac{19}{7} + \\frac{30}{7} = \\frac{49}{7} = 7\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(2x - y = 4\\)\n\\[2\\left(\\frac{19}{7}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) = \\frac{38}{7} - \\frac{10}{7} = \\frac{28}{7} = 4\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(x + 3y = 7\\) and \\(2x - y = 4\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the second equation, solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[2x - y = 4\\]\n\\[2x = 4 + y\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{4 + y}{2}\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = \\frac{4 + y}{2}\\) into the first equation:\n\\[x + 3y = 7\\]\n\\[\\frac{4 + y}{2} + 3y = 7\\]\n\\[\\frac{4 + y + 6y}{2} = 7\\]\n\\[\\frac{4 + 7y}{2} = 7\\]\n\\[4 + 7y = 14\\]\n\\[7y = 14 - 4\\]\n\\[7y = 10\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{10}{7}\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y = \\frac{10}{7}\\) back into \\(x = \\frac{4 + y}{2}\\):\n\\[x = \\frac{4 + \\frac{10}{7}}{2}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{\\frac{28}{7} + \\frac{10}{7}}{2}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{\\frac{38}{7}}{2}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{38}{14}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{19}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{19}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{10}{7}\\).\n\nTo verify:\n1. \\(x + 3y = 7\\)\n\\[\\frac{19}{7} + 3\\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) = \\frac{19}{7} + \\frac{30}{7} = \\frac{49}{7} = 7\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(2x - y = 4\\)\n\\[2\\left(\\frac{19}{7}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{10}{7}\\right) = \\frac{38}{7} - \\frac{10}{7} = \\frac{28}{7} = 4\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed examples. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to further practice the substitution method?\n<think>\nCertainly! Let's consider another system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(x - 2y = -1\\)\n2. \\(3x + y = 8\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the first equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x - 2y = -1\\]\n\\[x = -1 + 2y\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(x\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[3x + y = 8\\]\n\\[3(-1 + 2y) + y = 8\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[3(-1) + 6y + y = 8\\]\n\\[-3 + 7y = 8\\]\n\\[7y = 8 + 3\\]\n\\[7y = 11\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{11}{7}\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = \\frac{11}{7}\\) back into \\(x = -1 + 2y\\):\n\n\\[x = -1 + 2\\left(\\frac{11}{7}\\right)\\]\n\\[x = -1 + \\frac{22}{7}\\]\n\\[x = -\\frac{7}{7} + \\frac{22}{7}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{15}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{15}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{11}{7}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(x - 2y = -1\\)\n\\[\\frac{15}{7} - 2\\left(\\frac{11}{7}\\right) = \\frac{15}{7} - \\frac{22}{7} = \\frac{-7}{7} = -1\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(3x + y = 8\\)\n\\[3\\left(\\frac{15}{7}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{11}{7}\\right) = \\frac{45}{7} + \\frac{11}{7} = \\frac{56}{7} = 8\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(x - 2y = -1\\) and \\(3x + y = 8\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the first equation, solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[x - 2y = -1\\]\n\\[x = -1 + 2y\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = -1 + 2y\\) into the second equation:\n\\[3x + y = 8\\]\n\\[3(-1 + 2y) + y = 8\\]\n\\[3(-1) + 6y + y = 8\\]\n\\[-3 + 7y = 8\\]\n\\[7y = 8 + 3\\]\n\\[7y = 11\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{11}{7}\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y = \\frac{11}{7}\\) back into \\(x = -1 + 2y\\):\n\\[x = -1 + 2\\left(\\frac{11}{7}\\right)\\]\n\\[x = -1 + \\frac{22}{7}\\]\n\\[x = -\\frac{7}{7} + \\frac{22}{7}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{15}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{15}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{11}{7}\\).\n\nTo verify:\n1. \\(x - 2y = -1\\)\n\\[\\frac{15}{7} - 2\\left(\\frac{11}{7}\\right) = \\frac{15}{7} - \\frac{22}{7} = \\frac{-7}{7} = -1\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(3x + y = 8\\)\n\\[3\\left(\\frac{15}{7}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{11}{7}\\right) = \\frac{45}{7} + \\frac{11}{7} = \\frac{56}{7} = 8\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed examples. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to further practice the substitution method?\n<think>\nOf course! Let's consider another system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(2x + 3y = 11\\)\n2. \\(x - y = 1\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the second equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x - y = 1\\]\n\\[x = 1 + y\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(x\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[2x + 3y = 11\\]\n\\[2(1 + y) + 3y = 11\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[2 + 2y + 3y = 11\\]\n\\[2 + 5y = 11\\]\n\\[5y = 11 - 2\\]\n\\[5y = 9\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{9}{5}\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = \\frac{9}{5}\\) back into \\(x = 1 + y\\):\n\n\\[x = 1 + \\frac{9}{5}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{5}{5} + \\frac{9}{5}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{14}{5}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{14}{5}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{9}{5}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(2x + 3y = 11\\)\n\\[2\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right) + 3\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) = \\frac{28}{5} + \\frac{27}{5} = \\frac{55}{5} = 11\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x - y = 1\\)\n\\[\\frac{14}{5} - \\frac{9}{5} = \\frac{5}{5} = 1\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(2x + 3y = 11\\) and \\(x - y = 1\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the second equation, solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[x - y = 1\\]\n\\[x = 1 + y\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = 1 + y\\) into the first equation:\n\\[2x + 3y = 11\\]\n\\[2(1 + y) + 3y = 11\\]\n\\[2 + 2y + 3y = 11\\]\n\\[2 + 5y = 11\\]\n\\[5y = 11 - 2\\]\n\\[5y = 9\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{9}{5}\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y = \\frac{9}{5}\\) back into \\(x = 1 + y\\):\n\\[x = 1 + \\frac{9}{5}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{5}{5} + \\frac{9}{5}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{14}{5}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{14}{5}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{9}{5}\\).\n\nTo verify:\n1. \\(2x + 3y = 11\\)\n\\[2\\left(\\frac{14}{5}\\right) + 3\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) = \\frac{28}{5} + \\frac{27}{5} = \\frac{55}{5} = 11\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(x - y = 1\\)\n\\[\\frac{14}{5} - \\frac{9}{5} = \\frac{5}{5} = 1\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed examples. Can you provide one more example with a different system of equations to further practice the substitution method?\n<think>\nOf course! Let's consider another system of linear equations:\n\n1. \\(x + 2y = 5\\)\n2. \\(3x - y = 7\\)\n\nWe'll use the substitution method to solve this system. Let's start by solving the first equation for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x + 2y = 5\\]\n\\[x = 5 - 2y\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(x\\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[3x - y = 7\\]\n\\[3(5 - 2y) - y = 7\\]\n\nNext, simplify and solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[15 - 6y - y = 7\\]\n\\[15 - 7y = 7\\]\n\\[-7y = 7 - 15\\]\n\\[-7y = -8\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{8}{7}\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = \\frac{8}{7}\\) back into \\(x = 5 - 2y\\):\n\n\\[x = 5 - 2\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\\]\n\\[x = 5 - \\frac{16}{7}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{35}{7} - \\frac{16}{7}\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{19}{7}\\]\n\nSo, the solution to the system of equations is \\(x = \\frac{19}{7}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{8}{7}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can substitute these values back into the original equations:\n\n1. \\(x + 2y = 5\\)\n\\[\\frac{19}{7} + 2\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right) = \\frac{19}{7} + \\frac{16}{7} = \\frac{35}{7} = 5\\] (True)\n\n2. \\(3x - y = 7\\)\n\\[3\\left(\\frac{19}{7}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right) = \\frac{57}{7} - \\frac{8}{7} = \\frac{49}{7} = 7\\] (True)\n\nTherefore, the solution is correct.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's solve the system of equations \\(x + 2y = 5\\) and \\(3x - y = 7\\) using the substitution method:\n\n1. From the first equation, solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[x + 2y = 5\\]\n\\[x = 5 - 2y\\]\n\n2. Substitute \\(x = 5 - 2y\\) into the second equation:\n\\[3x - y = 7\\]\n\\[3(5 - 2y) - y = 7\\]\n\\[15 - 6y - y = 7\\]\n\\[15 - 7y = 7\\]\n\\[-7y = 7 - 15\\]\n\\[-7y = -8\\]\n\\[y = \\frac{8}{7}\\]\n\n3. Substitute \\(y = \\frac{8}{7}\\) back into \\(x = 5 - 2y\\):\n\\[x = 5 - 2\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\rig