s our previous result. The distributive property allows us to simplify the multiplication process by breaking it down into smaller, more manageable parts. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's very helpful. Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the area model method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The area model method is a visual way to break down multiplication into smaller parts, similar to the distributive property. We can represent 216 \u00d7 124 as a rectangle with dimensions 216 and 124. We'll divide each number into its place values and then create smaller rectangles to represent each part of the multiplication:\n1. Break down 216 into 200, 10, and 6\n2. Break down 124 into 100, 20, and 4\nNow, we'll create a grid with these divisions and calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n```\n  200 | 10 |  6\n+-----+----+----+\n100  | 200| 100|  60\n+-----+----+----+\n20   | 200| 100|  60\n+-----+----+----+\n4    | 200| 100|  60\n```\nThe area of each smaller rectangle represents the product of the corresponding place values. We'll add up the areas of all the smaller rectangles to get the total product:\n```\n  200 \u00d7 200 = 40000\n  100 \u00d7 200 = 20000\n   60 \u00d7 200 = 12000\n  200 \u00d7 100 = 20000\n  100 \u00d7 100 = 10000\n   60 \u00d7 100 = 6000\n  200 \u00d7  4 =  800\n  100 \u00d7  4 =  400\n   60 \u00d7  4 =  240\n  4 \u00d7  4 =   16\n```\nAdding these areas together:\n```\n40000 + 20000 + 12000 + 20000 + 10000 + 6000 + 800 + 400 + 240 + 16 = 26784\n```\nTherefore, the product of 216 and 124 is 26784.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're absolutely right. The area model method visually represents the multiplication by breaking it down into smaller, more manageable parts, similar to the distributive property. By creating a grid and calculating the area of each smaller rectangle, we can easily find the total product. The final answer is 26784. Thank you for explaining this method in detail. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the partial products method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The partial products method is another way to break down multiplication into smaller parts. We'll multiply 216 by 124 by breaking it down into the products of its place values and then adding those products together. Let's break down 216 into 200, 10, and 6, and 124 into 100, 20, and 4. We'll then multiply each part of 216 by each part of 124 and add the results:\n1. Multiply 200 by 100: 200 \u00d7 100 = 20000\n2. Multiply 200 by 20: 200 \u00d7 20 = 4000\n3. Multiply 200 by 4: 200 \u00d7 4 = 800\n4. Multiply 10 by 100: 10 \u00d7 100 = 1000\n5. Multiply 10 by 20: 10 \u00d7 20 = 200\n6. Multiply 10 by 4: 10 \u00d7 4 = 40\n7. Multiply 6 by 100: 6 \u00d7 100 = 600\n8. Multiply 6 by 20: 6 \u00d7 20 = 120\n9. Multiply 6 by 4: 6 \u00d7 4 = 24\nNow, we'll add all these partial products together:\n```\n20000\n+ 4000\n+  800\n+ 1000\n+  200\n+  40\n+  600\n+ 120\n+  24\n------\n 26784\n```\nTherefore, the product of 216 and 124 is 26784.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>The partial products method involves breaking down the multiplication into smaller parts by multiplying each place value of one number by each place value of the other number. We then add up all the partial products to get the final result. In this case, the partial products are 20000, 4000, 800, 1000, 200, 40, 600, 120, and 24, and their sum is 26784. This method provides a clear and systematic way to solve the multiplication problem. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the standard long multiplication method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The standard long multiplication method involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, starting from the rightmost digit, and then adding up the results. Let's multiply 216 by 124 using the standard long multiplication method:\n```\n      216\n\u00d7    124\n------\n      864  (216 \u00d7 4)\n     8640  (216 \u00d7 20, shifted one place to the left)\n    43200  (216 \u00d7 100, shifted two places to the left)\n------\n    26784\n```\nWe'll start by multiplying 216 by 4 (the units digit of 124):\n```\n      216\n\u00d7      4\n------\n      864\n```\nNext, we'll multiply 216 by 20 (the tens digit of 124), but remember to shift the result one place to the left:\n```\n      216\n\u00d7     20\n------\n     4320\n```\nFinally, we'll multiply 216 by 100 (the hundreds digit of 124), but remember to shift the result two places to the left:\n```\n      216\n\u00d7    100\n------\n    43200\n```\nNow, we'll add these results together:\n```\n      864\n+  43200\n+  26784\n------\n    26784\n```\nTherefore, the product of 216 and 124 is 26784.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>The standard long multiplication method involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, starting from the rightmost digit, and then adding up the results. In this case, we multiplied 216 by 4 to get 864, then multiplied 216 by 20 to get 4320, and finally multiplied 216 by 100 to get 43200. Adding these results together, we get the final product of 26784. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the lattice method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The lattice method is a visual way to perform multiplication using a grid. We'll use a 3x3 grid to represent the multiplication of 216 by 124. Here's how we'll set it up:\n```\n  216\n\u00d7 124\n------\n  864\n  864\n 4320\n------\n 26784\n```\nWe'll start by multiplying each digit of 216 by each digit of 124 and placing the results in the appropriate cells of the grid. Then, we'll add up the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over as necessary. Let's break it down step by step:\n1. Multiply 2 by 1: 2 \u00d7 1 = 2\n2. Multiply 2 by 2: 2 \u00d7 2 = 4\n3. Multiply 2 by 4: 2 \u00d7 4 = 8\n4. Multiply 1 by 1: 1 \u00d7 1 = 1\n5. Multiply 1 by 2: 1 \u00d7 2 = 2\n6. Multiply 1 by 4: 1 \u00d7 4 = 4\n7. Multiply 6 by 1: 6 \u00d7 1 = 6\n8. Multiply 6 by 2: 6 \u00d7 2 = 12\n9. Multiply 6 by 4: 6 \u00d7 4 = 24\nNow, we'll place these results in the appropriate cells of the grid:\n```\n  216\n\u00d7 124\n------\n  864\n  864\n 4320\n------\n 26784\n```\nWe'll add up the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over as necessary:\n```\n  2\n  4\n  8\n 1\n 2\n 4\n 6\n 1\n 2\n------\n 26784\n```\nTherefore, the product of 216 and 124 is 26784.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>The lattice method involves setting up a grid and multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, placing the results in the appropriate cells, and then adding up the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over as necessary. In this case, we set up a 3x3 grid and multiplied each digit of 216 by each digit of 124, placing the results in the appropriate cells. We then added up the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over as necessary, to get the final product of 26784. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the grid method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The grid method, also known as the box method, involves breaking down the numbers into their place values and then multiplying each part of one number by each part of the other number. We'll use a 2x2 grid to represent the multiplication of 216 by 124. Here's how we'll set it up:\n```\n  216\n\u00d7 124\n------\n  864\n  864\n 4320\n------\n 26784\n```\nWe'll start by breaking down 216 into 200, 10, and 6, and 124 into 100, 20, and 4. We'll then multiply each part of 216 by each part of 124 and place the results in the appropriate cells of the grid:\n```\n  200 | 10 |  6\n+-----+----+----+\n100  | 200| 100|  60\n+-----+----+----+\n20   | 200| 100|  60\n+-----+----+----+\n4    | 200| 100|  60\n```\nNow, we'll calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n```\n  200 \u00d7 200 = 40000\n  100 \u00d7 200 = 20000\n   60 \u00d7 200 = 12000\n  200 \u00d7 100 = 20000\n  100 \u00d7 100 = 10000\n   60 \u00d7 100 = 6000\n  200 \u00d7  4 =  800\n  100 \u00d7  4 =  400\n   60 \u00d7  4 =  240\n  4 \u00d7  4 =   16\n```\nFinally, we'll add up the areas of all the smaller rectangles to get the total product:\n```\n40000 + 20000 + 12000 + 20000 + 10000 + 6000 + 800 + 400 + 240 + 16 = 26784\n```\nTherefore, the product of 216 and 124 is 26784.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>The grid method involves breaking down the numbers into their place values and then multiplying each part of one number by each part of the other number, placing the results in a grid. In this case, we broke down 216 into 200, 10, and 6, and 124 into 100, 20, and 4. We then multiplied each part of 216 by each part of 124 and placed the results in the appropriate cells of the grid. We calculated the area of each smaller rectangle and added up the areas to get the total product of 26784. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the area model method, but this time using a different approach?\n<think>\nCertainly! The area model method can be visualized using a rectangle where the length and width represent the two numbers being multiplied. We'll use the same numbers, 216 and 124, but we'll break down the process differently. Let's start by drawing a rectangle and dividing it into smaller rectangles based on the place values of the numbers:\n```\n  216\n\u00d7 124\n------\n  864\n  864\n 4320\n------\n 26784\n```\nWe'll divide the rectangle into four smaller rectangles:\n1. The top-left rectangle represents 200 \u00d7 100\n2. The top-right rectangle represents 200 \u00d7 20\n3. The bottom-left rectangle represents 10 \u00d7 100\n4. The bottom-right rectangle represents 10 \u00d7 20\nNow, we'll calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n```\n  200 \u00d7 100 = 20000\n  200 \u00d7 20 = 4000\n  10 \u00d7 100 = 1000\n  10 \u00d7 20 = 200\n```\nFinally, we'll add up the areas of all the smaller rectangles to get the total product:\n```\n20000 + 4000 + 1000 + 200 = 26200\n```\nHowever, we missed the remaining parts of the original multiplication. Let's add those parts:\n```\n  200 \u00d7 4 = 800\n  10 \u00d7 4 = 40\n  100 \u00d7 4 = 400\n  200 \u00d7 4 = 800\n  100 \u00d7 4 = 400\n  60 \u00d7 4 = 240\n  200 \u00d7 4 = 800\n  100 \u00d7 4 = 400\n  60 \u00d7 4 = 240\n  4 \u00d7 4 = 16\n```\nAdding these parts to the previous total:\n```\n26200 + 800 + 40 + 400 + 800 + 400 + 240 + 800 + 400 + 240 + 16 = 26784\n```\nTherefore, the product of 216 and 124 is 26784.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>The area model method can be visualized using a rectangle where the length and width represent the two numbers being multiplied. We'll divide the rectangle into smaller rectangles based on the place values of the numbers. In this case, we divided the rectangle into four smaller rectangles:\n1. The top-left rectangle represents 200 \u00d7 100\n2. The top-right rectangle represents 200 \u00d7 20\n3. The bottom-left rectangle represents 10 \u00d7 100\n4. The bottom-right rectangle represents 10 \u00d7 20\nWe calculated the area of each smaller rectangle and added up the areas to get the total product of 26200. However, we missed the remaining parts of the original multiplication. We added those parts:\n```\n  200 \u00d7 4 = 800\n  10 \u00d7 4 = 40\n  100 \u00d7 4 = 400\n  200 \u00d7 4 = 800\n  100 \u00d7 4 = 400\n  60 \u00d7 4 = 240\n  200 \u00d7 4 = 800\n  100 \u00d7 4 = 400\n  60 \u00d7 4 = 240\n  4 \u00d7 4 = 16\n```\nAdding these parts to the previous total:\n```\n26200 + 800 + 40 + 400 + 800 + 400 + 240 + 800 + 400 + 240 + 16 = 26784\n```\nTherefore, the product of 216 and 124 is 26784. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you also provide an example of how to solve this problem using the area model method, but this time using a different approach?\n<think>\nCertainly! Let's use a different approach to solve the problem using the area model method. We'll break down the numbers into their place values and then create a grid to represent the multiplication. Here's how we'll set it up:\n```\n  216\n\u00d7 124\n------\n  864\n 