ying larger numbers by breaking them down into smaller, more manageable parts. For the problem 718 x 898, we can use the partial products method by multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number and then summing the results.\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Multiply 718 by 800:**\n   - 718 x 800 = 718 x (8 x 100) = 718 x 8 x 100\n   - First, multiply 718 by 8:\n     ```\n     718\n   x   8\n   -----\n     5744\n     ```\n   - Then, add three zeros to the right:\n     ```\n     718\n   x   8\n   -----\n     57440\n     ```\n   - So, 718 x 800 = 574400\n\n2. **Multiply 718 by 90:**\n   - 718 x 90 = 718 x (9 x 10) = 718 x 9 x 10\n   - First, multiply 718 by 9:\n     ```\n     718\n   x   9\n   -----\n     6462\n     ```\n   - Then, add one zero to the right:\n     ```\n     718\n   x   9\n   -----\n     64620\n     ```\n   - So, 718 x 90 = 64620\n\n3. **Multiply 718 by 8:**\n   - 718 x 8 = 718 x 8\n   - First, multiply 718 by 8:\n     ```\n     718\n   x   8\n   -----\n     5744\n     ```\n   - So, 718 x 8 = 5744\n\nNow, we sum all the partial products:\n```\n574400 (718 x 800)\n+64620  (718 x 90)\n+5744   (718 x 8)\n------\n 634884\n```\nTherefore, using the partial products method, we find that:\n```\n718 x 898 = 634884\n```\nThis method breaks down the multiplication into smaller, simpler multiplications and then sums the results, making it easier to handle larger numbers. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation using the partial products method. Can you provide an example of how to use the standard algorithm to solve this multiplication problem?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! The standard algorithm is the traditional method for multiplying two numbers by aligning them vertically and then multiplying each digit step by step. Let's solve 718 x 898 using the standard algorithm:\n\n1. **Write the numbers vertically:**\n   ```\n     718\n   x 898\n   ------\n   ```\n\n2. **Multiply 718 by 8 (the units digit of 898