he multiplication:\n\\[\n798 \\times 557 = (800 - 2) \\times (500 + 50 + 7)\n\\]\n\nWe can distribute \\(800\\) and \\(-2\\) across the terms in the second expression:\n\\[\n(800 \\times 500) + (800 \\times 50) + (800 \\times 7) - (2 \\times 500) - (2 \\times 50) - (2 \\times 7)\n\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate each term separately:\n\\[\n800 \\times 500 = 400000\n\\]\n\\[\n800 \\times 50 = 40000\n\\]\n\\[\n800 \\times 7 = 5600\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\times 500 = 1000\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\times 50 = 100\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\times 7 = 14\n\\]\n\nNow, we combine all these results:\n\\[\n400000 + 40000 + 5600 - 1000 - 100 - 14\n\\]\n\nLet's add and subtract these step by step:\n\\[\n400000 + 40000 = 440000\n\\]\n\\[\n440000 + 5600 = 445600\n\\]\n\\[\n445600 - 1000 = 444600\n\\]\n\\[\n444600 - 100 = 444500\n\\]\n\\[\n444500 - 14 = 444486\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 798 and 557 using the distributive property is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{444486}\n\\]\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>444486<|User|>You're absolutely right. The correct answer using the distributive property is 444486. Thank you for walking me through this detailed process.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you understand the process using the distributive property. If you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask. <|User|>You're welcome! I have one more question. How would you solve the same problem using the distributive property if the numbers were 798 and 558 instead?\n<think>\nTo solve the problem \\(798 \\times 558\\) using the distributive property, we can break down the numbers into their components and then apply the distributive property. Let's express 798 and 558 in terms of their components:\n- \\(798 = 800 - 2\\)\n- \\(558 = 500 + 50 + 8\\)\n\nNow, we can use the distributive property to expand the multiplication:\n\\[\n798 \\times 558 = (800 - 2) \\times (500 + 50 + 8)\n\\]\n\nWe can distribute \\(800\\) and \\(-2\\) across the terms in the second expression:\n\\[\n(800 \\times 500) + (80