r**:\n   - (500 \u00d7 900) + (500 \u00d7 1) + (6 \u00d7 900) + (6 \u00d7 1)\n\n3. **Calculate each product**:\n   - 500 \u00d7 900 = 450,000\n   - 500 \u00d7 1 = 500\n   - 6 \u00d7 900 = 5,400\n   - 6 \u00d7 1 = 6\n\n4. **Sum the results**:\n   ```\n   450,000\n   + 500\n   + 5,400\n   +  6\n   ------\n   455,906\n   ```\n\nTherefore, using the partial products method, we find that 506 \u00d7 901 = 455,906.\n\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation using the partial products method. It's very helpful.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the explanation helpful. If you have any more questions or need further assistance, don't hesitate to ask. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you also show me how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The standard algorithm method involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, starting from the rightmost digit, and then summing the results. For 506 \u00d7 901, we'll align the numbers vertically and multiply each digit step by step.\n...\n<answer>\nCertainly! The standard algorithm method involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, starting from the rightmost digit, and then summing the results. For 506 \u00d7 901, we'll align the numbers vertically and multiply each digit step by step.\n\nLet's set up the multiplication:\n\n```\n    506\n  \u00d7 901\n  ------\n    506  (506 \u00d7 1)\n   5060  (506 \u00d7 0, shifted one place to the left)\n  45540  (506 \u00d7 9, shifted two places to the left)\n  ------\n  455906\n```\n\nHere's the step-by-step breakdown:\n\n1. **Multiply 506 by 1 (the units digit of 901)**:\n   ```\n   506 \u00d7 1 = 506\n   ```\n\n2. **Multiply 506 by 0 (the tens digit of 901), and shift the result one place to the left**:\n   ```\n   506 \u00d7 0 = 0\n  