4 \\times 100 = 56400\\)\n2. \\(564 \\times 10 = 5640\\)\n3. \\(564 \\times 1 = 564\\)\n\nNow, we add these results together:\n\n\\[56400 + 5640 + 564 = 62584\\]\n\nSo, using the distributive property, we get the same result:\n\n\\[564 \\times 111 = 62584\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 564 and 111 is 62,584. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's very clear. Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the area model for multiplication?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\nCertainly! The area model for multiplication is a visual method that breaks down the multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts. For the multiplication of 564 by 111, we can break it down into the areas of rectangles formed by the digits of the numbers.\n\nHere's how we can set up the area model:\n\n1. Break down 564 into 500, 60, and 4.\n2. Break down 111 into 100, 10, and 1.\n\nNow, we can create a grid where each row represents one of the parts of 564 and each column represents one of the parts of 111. The area of each small rectangle in the grid will be the product of the corresponding parts, and the total area of the grid will be the product of 564 and 111.\n\nLet's set up the grid:\n\n```\n  500 | 60  |  4\n+-----------------\n100 | 5000| 600  |  40\n+-----------------\n 10 | 500  | 60   |  4\n+-----------------\n 1 | 50   | 6    |  4\n+-----------------\n```\n\nNow, we calculate the area of each small rectangle:\n\n1. \\(500 \\times 100 = 50000\\)\n2. \\(500 \\times 10 = 5000\\)\n3. \\(500 \\times 1 = 500\\)\n4. \\(60 \\times 100 = 6000\\)\n5. \\(60 \\times 10 = 600\\)\n6. \\(60 \\times 1 = 60\\)\n7. \\(4 \\times 100 = 400\\)\n8. \\(4 \\times 10 = 40\\)\n9. \\(4 \\times 1 = 4\\)\n\nNow, we sum these areas:\n\n\\[50000 + 5000 + 500 + 6000 + 600 + 60 + 400 + 40 + 4 = 62584\\]\n\nSo, using the area model, we get the same result:\n\n\\[564 \\times 111 = 62584\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 564 and 111 is 62,584. <\uff5c>",
