e values**:\n   - 645 can be broken down into 600, 40, and 5.\n   - 732 can be broken down into 700, 30, and 2.\n\n2. **Create a 3x3 grid and place the decomposed numbers along the top and side of the grid**:\n```\n  600 | 40 |  5\n------+-----+---\n 700 | 30 |  2\n```\n\n3. **Multiply each pair of numbers from the top and side to fill in the grid**:\n   - \\(600 \\times 700 = 420,000\\)\n   - \\(600 \\times 30 = 18,000\\)\n   - \\(600 \\times 2 = 1,200\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 700 = 28,000\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 30 = 1,200\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 2 = 80\\)\n   - \\(5 \\times 700 = 3,500\\)\n   - \\(5 \\times 30 = 150\\)\n   - \\(5 \\times 2 = 10\\)\n\n4. **Sum the areas of the individual rectangles to get the final product**:\n   \\[\n   420,000 + 18,000 + 1,200 + 28,000 + 1,200 + 80 + 3,500 + 150 + 10 = 469,340\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 645 and 732 using the area model is \\(\\boxed{469340}\\).<|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation and steps for the area model. Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method?\n<think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication of 645 and 732 using the partial products method step by step:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:\n   - 645 can be broken down into 600, 40, and 5.\n   - 732 can be broken down into 700, 30, and 2.\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:\n   - \\(600 \\times 700 = 420,000\\)\n   - \\(600 \\times 30 = 18,000\\)\n   - \\(600 \\times 2 = 1,200\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 700 = 28,000\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 30 = 1,200\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 2 = 80\\)\n   - \\(5 \\times 700 = 3,500\\)\n   - \\(5 \\times 30 = 150\\)\n   - \\(5 \\times 2 = 10\\)\n\n3. **Sum the partial products**:\n   \\[\n   420,000 + 18,000 + 1,200 + 28,000 + 1,200 + 80 + 3,500 + 150 + 10 = 469,340\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 645 and 732 using the partial products method is \\(\\boxed{469340}\\).<|User|>Thank you for the detaile