uct:**\n   - \\(900 \\times 1000 = 900000\\)\n   - \\(900 \\times (-12) = -10800\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 1000 = 40000\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times (-12) = -480\\)\n   - \\(7 \\times 1000 = 7000\\)\n   - \\(7 \\times (-12) = -84\\)\n\n4. **Sum all the partial products:**\n   \\[\n   900000 + (-10800) + 40000 + (-480) + 7000 + (-84)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the sum:**\n   \\[\n   900000 - 10800 + 40000 - 480 + 7000 - 84\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 900000 + 40000 + 7000 - 10800 - 480 - 84\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 947000 - 11364\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 935636\n   \\]\n\nSo, using the partial products method, we find that:\n\\[ 947 \\times 988 = 935636 \\]\n\nThis method breaks down the multiplication into smaller, more straightforward steps, making it easier to manage and understand, especially for larger numbers. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed example using the partial products method. This is a great way to break down the multiplication process.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the example helpful. The partial products method is a systematic approach that can make complex multiplications more accessible and easier to understand. If you have any more questions or need further clarification on this or any other topic, feel free to ask. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you provide an example of how to use the standard algorithm to solve a multiplication problem like this?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! The standard algorithm is the most common and straightforward method for multiplying larger numbers. Let's use the numbers 947 and 988 to demonstrate how to use the standard algorithm.\n\n### Step-by-Step Standard Algorithm Example\n\n1. **Write the numbers one above the other:**\n   ```\n     947\n   \u00d7 988\n   ```\n\n2. **Multiply 947 by the units digit of 988 (which is 8):**\n   - First, multiply 947 by 8:\n     ```\n       947\n     \u00d7   8\n     ------\n       7576\n     ```\n\n3. **Multiply 947 by the tens digit of 988 (which is 80, or 8 \u00d